{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Los Angeles Dodgers announced themselves immediately, racing through March with a perfect 6-0 record powered by a lineup that looked capable of scoring in bunches every single night. Teoscar Hernández's three-run blast on Opening Day set the tone, and even when the bullpen wobbled — as it would throughout the year — hitters like Will Smith, Max Muncy, and Shohei Ohtani kept finding answers. April and May were steady if unspectacular, as the Dodgers built a winning rhythm despite closer Tanner Scott proving to be one of the most maddening storylines of the summer, surrendering crucial home runs and clutch hits at the worst possible moments.\n\nJune represented the team's clearest statement of intent. A 17-10 record made it the best full month of the season, fueled by Muncy's grand slam, a go-ahead Smith homer, and Tanner Scott's bullpen redemption in the form of inducing a game-ending double play against Kansas City. The offense was humming, the rotation was holding, and Los Angeles looked every bit like a World Series favorite. Then July arrived and the wheels wobbled — a 10-14 month that could easily have derailed the whole enterprise. Tanner Scott continued to bleed games, yet Freddie Freeman delivered the season's single most valuable hit on July 23rd, a line drive to left that plated three runners and rescued a game that seemed lost.\n\nAugust and September saw the Dodgers grind back to winning records, steadied by milestone moments: Andy Pages slugging his 25th home run in a pennant-race spot, Shohei Ohtani reaching 52 home runs on September 19th — a breathtaking individual achievement woven into a team victory. Scott continued to haunt the bullpen ledger with damaging outings deep into September, but the roster's collective depth absorbed every setback. At 93-69, Los Angeles entered October as a battle-tested champion-in-waiting rather than a pristine favorite.\n\nThe postseason was a coronation earned through adversity. Hernández's three-run shot ignited the Division Series, and the Dodgers dispatched each opponent with a blend of clutch hitting and lockdown pitching. By the World Series against Toronto, the Dodgers were playing their best baseball: Freeman homering in Game 1, Tyler Glasnow conjuring a pivotal double play in Game 5, and on November 1st Miguel Rojas and Will Smith going back-to-back while Yoshinobu Yamamoto induced a clinching double play to seal Los Angeles's championship. It was a season that tested every roster depth chart and every bullpen phone call — and in the end, the Dodgers answered every test.",
